2186528450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529079 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529079 #edb/ref 1077 "field-8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529079 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529079 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eff6a071-b750-583c-a855-cd02b33251a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186528720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1077 "field-7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529086 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ff4301ae-bf90-5f16-a0cd-99a316c4b8d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529096 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529096 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529096 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529096 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.56 & -0.17 & 0.71 & -0.393 \\\\ 0.682 & -0.53 & 0.486 & 0.135 \\\\ 0.264 & 0.821 & 0.485 & 0.144 \\\\ -0.39 & -0.126 & 0.159 & 0.898\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.085 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.292 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & 0.708 \\\\ 0.708 & -0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17f34adc-72ed-5271-a021-3a7f1d773e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529096 #edb/ref 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529104 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.27 & 0.39 \\\\ -0.99 & -1.23 \\\\ -0.13 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.39 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.035 & 0.86 & 0.132 & 0.491 \\\\ -0.657 & -0.314 & -0.208 & 0.653 \\\\ -0.121 & -0.196 & 0.969 & 0.092 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.351 & -0.032 & 0.569\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.389 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.542 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ -0.657 \\\\ -0.121 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ 0.86 \\\\ 0.132 \\\\ 0.491\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2633d33a-0706-5b9b-a985-6a0050cda4a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529104 #edb/ref 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III uses the values of the first row of $U$ multiplied by ${\\sigma }_{1}.$ However, the projection corresponds to the first column of $U.$ Thus, using the row vector is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529109 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529109 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529109 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.74 & 0.61 & 0.89 \\\\ 0.38 & 0 & 0.61 \\\\ -0.92 & -0.03 & -0.87 \\\\ -1.2 & -1.61 & -1.28 \\\\ 1.01 & 1.02 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.389 & 0.167 & -0.312 & 0.84 & -0.133 \\\\ -0.174 & 0.453 & -0.384 & -0.192 & 0.762 \\\\ 0.321 & -0.749 & -0.348 & 0.232 & 0.402 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.384 & 0.359 & 0.419 & 0.22 \\\\ -0.464 & -0.242 & 0.712 & 0.166 & 0.438\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.329 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.311 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.591 & 0.31 & 0.745 \\\\ -0.558 & -0.823 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.582 & 0.475 & -0.66\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529109 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529109 #edb/ref 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.329,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=0.9,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.311.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈92.4%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈99.2%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $92.4%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $99.2%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/e2/e2af29d0509f7ae3e3826cd35d3190b478e9c2415c00fc0e3798800d33381c59.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95%$ of the variance." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529111 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529111 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 & 0.813 & 0.505 & 0.021 \\\\ -0.469 & -0.212 & 0.634 & -0.578 \\\\ 0.676 & -0.54 & 0.473 & 0.168 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.061 & 0.345 & 0.798\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.065 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.316 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & -0.705 \\\\ 0.705 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ -0.469 \\\\ 0.676 \\\\ -0.49\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.065$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3689d124-7069-5ac1-b495-7dcfa807134a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529111 #edb/ref 1074 "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529116 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529116 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529116 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529116 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529116 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.36 & 1.7 & 0.25 & 1.05 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.33 & -1.37 & -0.53 \\\\ -0.51 & -0.23 & 0.05 & -0.61 \\\\ -1.18 & -0.96 & -0.32 & -1.01 \\\\ 1.25 & -0.18 & 1.4 & 1.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.477 & 0.704 & -0.239 & 0.135 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.291 & -0.357 & -0.764 & 0.049 & 0.449 \\\\ -0.217 & 0.106 & 0.24 & -0.83 & 0.442 \\\\ -0.55 & 0.135 & 0.445 & 0.529 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.582 & -0.589 & 0.323 & 0.104 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.041 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.967 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.671 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.268 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.356 & -0.757 & -0.46 & -0.298 \\\\ 0.454 & 0.644 & -0.416 & -0.454 \\\\ 0.493 & -0.1 & 0.783 & -0.366 \\\\ 0.652 & 0.041 & -0.052 & 0.755\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529116 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529116 #edb/ref 1074 "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.041,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.967,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.671,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.268.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈57.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈82.1%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈99.6%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $57.9%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.1%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $99.6%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb43d1adcfa48f0fd66cd73ea05b40a9e937399457ba8614f380502c1c632dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529129 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529129 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529129 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529129 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begi